<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ralambo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ralambo was a king of Imerina in the central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, traditionally dated to around 1575–1610, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriamanelo">Andriamanelo</a>. Royal tradition credits him with giving the kingdom the name Imerina, introducing the royal talismans and the festival of the royal bath, and organising the population. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianjaka">Andrianjaka</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ralambo was born at the sacred highland village of Alasora to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriamanelo">Andriamanelo</a> and Queen Randapavola, the only one of their children to survive to adulthood. A popular legend makes his mother the daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vazimba">Vazimba</a> water goddess Ivorombe and tells that, after losing six children, she gave birth on the advice of an astrologer at Alasora rather than at her parents' village of Ambohidrabiby, in a house built to resemble a boat, on the first day of the first month (Alahamady), the most auspicious date for a sovereign's birth. Stories link his name to a wild boar (lambo), but it more probably comes from the zebu, also called lambo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ralambo moved his capital from Alasora to Ambohidrabiby, the former capital of his maternal grandfather King Rabiby. Oral history credits him with dividing the andriana nobility into four ranks, introducing circumcision and marriage between close kin among the Merina nobles, and the practice of polygamy; he took four wives, who bore him three daughters and twelve sons. He obtained the first firearms in Imerina through trade with coastal kingdoms, imposed a capitation tax called the vadin-aina and used it to raise the first standing royal army, with units of blacksmiths and silversmiths to equip it.</p><p>Tradition also credits him with the domestication of the wild zebu for food and with the fandroana festival held on his birthday, though some have suggested he only added practices to an older ritual. He gathered twelve powerful sampy, communal talismans, among them Kelimalaza, said to have been brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betsileo">Betsileo</a> country by a woman named Kalobe, and made them divine protectors of the ruler's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Legends tell of victories over raiders and of a trap that drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vazimba">Vazimba</a> king Andrianafovaratra into exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ralambo was buried in the stone tomb of King Rabiby at Ambohidrabiby, which still stands. According to a 19th-century source his death was mourned for a full year, and a royal mausoleum was built over the tomb, a custom that lasted until the end of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. The royal sampy he assembled were worshipped until Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-ii">Ranavalona II</a> is said to have burned them on her conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1869. He is often considered a key figure in the making of Merina cultural identity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ralambo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…compiled in the nineteenth century, credit Andriamanelo, conventionally dated to the mid-sixteenth century, with subduing the earlier Vazimba inhabitants with iron-tipped spears and founding the line of Merina kings, and his son <b>Ralambo</b> with giving the kingdom its name, its royal talismans and its customs of cattle sacrifice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
